Say which context a KVO observer registered with, and which one it was handed.

A DIAGNOSTIC, NOT A FIX. A KVO context is an arbitrary pointer the application gives us and we hand
back untouched, so if we ever hand back the WRONG one the application dereferences OUR mistake and
the crash looks entirely like its own.

Swift Publisher registers NSString CONTEXTS and passes them straight to a function that scans them:
its observer takes the sixth argument, the context, and calls binding2Value(NSString*) with it,
which is visible in three instructions of the disassembly. When that string is dead the application
dies inside +[NSScanner scannerWithString:]. So the first question is whether the pointer we
deliver is the pointer that was registered, and nothing could answer it.

Both KVO trace lines now carry the context, printed as a BARE POINTER and never dereferenced,
because a context need not be an object at all and a probe must not be able to crash what it
measures. The existing _CiderKVOText helper says as much about the keyPath above it.

WHAT IT SETTLED IMMEDIATELY, in task #249:
    CCSegmentedControl observes selectedObjects.textIncreseFontSize ... context=0x72fc5eb4e770
    notify observer=CCSegmentedControl keyPath=selectedObjects.textIncreseFontSize ... context=0x72fc5eb4e770
Registered and delivered are the same pointer, so we are NOT losing or swapping contexts, and that
hypothesis is dead. It also made the shape of the real defect visible: 17 of this application
registrations use a context inside the image, which is a constant string and immortal, 802 use a
HEAP pointer and 743 pass null. Exactly one of the 802 is dead by notification time.

